            directional_finding: 'Ketones reduced mitochondrial ROS-related responses and neuronal injury, associated with increased NADH oxidation rather than altered GSH.'
            assessment:
              study: 'Primary neuronal/isolated-mitochondrial experiments using beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate at 1 mM each during glutamate/calcium stress.'
              population: 'Acutely dissociated rat neocortical neurons and isolated mitochondria.'
              result: 'Ketones reduced mitochondrial ROS-related responses and neuronal injury, associated with increased NADH oxidation rather than altered GSH.'
              effect_magnitude: 'Study-specific direction retained; no pooled effect or effective food dose inferred.'
              evidence_summary: 'Ketones reduced mitochondrial ROS-related responses and neuronal injury, associated with increased NADH oxidation rather than altered GSH.'
              limitations: 'Mechanism engagement in a stress model; no ordinary-food effect, universal ketone response or human brain outcome.'
              retrieval_scope: 'Primary abstract and available full-text source sections examined; endpoint and formulation distinguished.'
              source_url: 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17240074/'
              publication_classification: 'primary-experimental'
        evidence_dependency:
          0: 'The formulation and measured endpoints are specific; linked publications or contextual diet studies are not independent replications.'
        connected_supportive_evidence:
          0:
            label: 'Jarrett et al. (2008)'
            why_relevant: 'Three-week ketogenic feeding increased hippocampal mitochondrial GSH and reduced H2O2 emission and oxidant-induced mtDNA damage in rats.'
            why_excluded: 'Does not isolate a ketone constituent, source-specific generation, or PM5 membrane/protein protection; mtDNA protection is not substituted for these endpoints.'
    scientific_findings_intro: 'Mitochondrial ROS generation is source- and state-dependent and can support adaptive signalling. Human feeding and activity experiments measured net H2O2 emission, with important assay and tissue limitations. No identifiable dietary input is newly admitted for source regulation. Fish-oil supplementation increased ROS-emission capacity without measured oxidative-damage increases; ketone mixtures altered stress responses in neuronal preparations. Neither establishes an ordinary-food prescription.'
