    scientific_findings_intro: 'Mitochondrial ROS generation is source- and state-dependent and can support adaptive signalling. Human feeding and activity experiments measured net H2O2 emission, with important assay and tissue limitations. No identifiable dietary input is newly admitted for source regulation. Fish-oil supplementation increased ROS-emission capacity without measured oxidative-damage increases; ketone mixtures altered stress responses in neuronal preparations. Neither establishes an ordinary-food prescription.'
