            exposure_context: 'Primary within-person leg immobilisation followed by supervised aerobic cycle retraining; isolated mitochondrial respirometry and H2O2 assays.'
            directional_finding: 'Immobilisation increased H2O2 emission and reduced ATP-generating respiration; subsequent training reversed these changes.'
            assessment:
              study: 'Primary within-person leg immobilisation followed by supervised aerobic cycle retraining; isolated mitochondrial respirometry and H2O2 assays.'
              population: 'Seventeen young and fifteen older healthy men.'
              result: 'Immobilisation increased H2O2 emission and reduced ATP-generating respiration; subsequent training reversed these changes.'
              effect_magnitude: 'Study-specific direction retained; no pooled estimate or ordinary-food effective dose inferred.'
              evidence_summary: 'Immobilisation increased H2O2 emission and reduced ATP-generating respiration; subsequent training reversed these changes.'
              limitations: 'Sequential design; skeletal muscle only; antioxidant-enzyme changes may influence emission.'
              retrieval_scope: 'Primary abstract retrieved from Europe PMC/PubMed; no unavailable full-method quantities inferred.'
