        record:
          id: 'brs4-pm4-pm4-f2-sec'
          recordVersion: 2
          pmId: 'BRS4-FM2-PM4'
          findingId: 'PM4-F2'
          title: 'Mitochondrial ROS can carry adaptive signals'
          proposition: 'Complex-III-dependent oxidant signals contribute to hypoxic HIF stabilisation in tested cell systems. Mitochondrial ROS therefore have physiological signalling roles as well as injury-associated roles.'
          assessmentStatus: 'codex-provisional'
          assessmentComplete: true
          lastEvidenceAssessmentDate: '2026-10-24'
          recordedOn: '2026-10-24'
          process: 'Codex bounded SEC follow-up v2: targeted primary Results/Methods recovery, exact-proposition precision and replication appraisal; no default factors, dietary inference or human review inferred.'
          methodology:
            name: 'Existing SEC four-factor anchors and integrated caps'
            version: '2026-10-24-pm4-pm5-followup-v2'
            source: 'system/reviews/sec-calibration/SEC-calibration-rubric-proposal.html#sec-assessment-status'
          factors:
            D: 2
            B: 1
            P: 1
            R: 1
          factorStates:
            D: 'established'
            B: 'established'
            P: 'established'
            R: 'established'
          rationale:
            D: 'Bell 2007 full primary experiments directly perturb complex-III Qo/Rieske signalling and measure ROS, HIF stabilisation and hydroxylation under hypoxia; this tests the existing cell-scoped proposition without relying solely on Guzy abstract data.'
            B: 'Bell Figures 1–8 include respiratory-incompetent cybrids, control shRNA, retained DMOG responses, loading controls, Qo versus Qi inhibitors, MitoQ versus TPMP and catalase controls. These address generalized respiratory failure and nonspecific ROS explanations; probe specificity, adapted cybrids and incomplete allocation/blinding reporting remain residual concerns (B1).'
            P: 'Bell Figures 1, 4, 5 and 8 report three/four independent experiments and mean/SEM for quantitative contrasts; loading/DMOG controls discriminate HIF signalling from wholesale response loss. These are informative for a qualitative contribution in tested cells; effect magnitude and model boundaries remain uncertain, warranting P1 rather than P2.'
            R: 'Guzy (Schumacker group) and Bell (Chandel group) provide separate empirical lines with non-overlapping author lists, but related 143B/cybrid/Rieske methods and contrary rho0-cell results (Srinivas 2001; Vaux 2001) limit generalisation. R1 credits partial confirmation, not universal oxygen-sensing necessity or a literature citation count.'
          rawScore: 5
          adjustedScore: 5
          capRationale: 'D2/B1/P1/R1 raw 5; no D1/B0/P0 cap applies; adjusted 5. No unapproved extra downgrade.'
          limitations:
            0: 'Hypoxia differs from anoxia; pharmacological respiratory inhibition can perturb multiple sites. This is not evidence for indiscriminate ROS reduction or a dietary-to-brain benefit.'
            1: 'Recover Guzy figures/methods and appraise control performance and uncertainty; compare Bell/Guzy model and research dependencies before a complete SEC.'
          sources:
            supporting:
              0: 'brs4_guzy2005_20261023'
            contextual:
            targetedChecks:
              0:
                doi: '10.1016/j.cmet.2005.05.001'
                role: 'supporting exact scoped proposition'
                access: 'Primary abstract retrieved from Europe PMC/PubMed; no unavailable full-method quantities inferred.'
                assessment: 'Complex-III function and oxidant signalling were required for the tested hypoxic HIF response. Context-dependent cell signalling; not all oxygen-sensing pathways or physiological states require this mechanism.'
              1:
                doi: '10.1083/jcb.200609074'
                role: 'supporting; pivotal full primary experiment for the same existing proposition'
                access: 'Complete Europe PMC XML: PMC2064363, figures/captions and Methods'
                assessment: 'Bell et al. (2007): Qo/Rieske perturbations, redox/HIF controls and independent repeats establish an informative scoped signalling contribution; not food or clinical efficacy.'
              2:
                doi: '10.1074/jbc.C100177200'
                role: 'contrasting; boundary on universal respiratory-chain/HIF dependence'
                access: 'Primary abstract, PMID11342528; original full Methods not appraised'
                assessment: 'Srinivas et al. (2001) observed hypoxic HIF responses in rho0 cells; retained as credible heterogeneity rather than excluded as an irrelevant negative.'
              3:
                doi: '10.1182/blood.V98.2.296'
                role: 'contrasting; oxygen-sensing model boundary'
                access: 'Primary Blood abstract, Vaux et al. (2001)'
                assessment: 'Hypoxic HIF responses persisted in respiratory-chain-defective cells; constrains any universal requirement interpretation.'
            retrievalLimited:
              0:
                url: 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cmet.2005.05.001'
                limitation: 'Guzy original full paper remains access-limited. Bell full primary evidence supplies the load-bearing appraisal for this cell-scoped synthesis; Guzy quantitative effects are not inferred.'
            contradictoryAssessment: 'Srinivas 2001 and Vaux 2001 report retained HIF responses in respiratory-chain-defective cells. These constrain universal necessity; the scored proposition explicitly concerns a contribution in the tested cell systems. Bell separates respiration from Qo redox signalling. Model/probe differences remain unresolved boundaries, not a reason to erase the contrary evidence.'
          evidenceLocations:
            0: 'Guzy 2005 primary abstract, PMID16054089'
            1: 'Bell 2007 primary abstract, PMID17562787 (targeted complementary check)'
            2: 'Bell 2007 PMC2064363: Figures 1–8 and cell/ROS/immunoblot Methods'
          history:
            0:
              date: '2026-10-24'
              decision: 'User authorised SEC completion for approved PM4/PM5 reassessment. New versioned assessment; propositions, admissions, original confidence storage and all prior SEC records preserved. This is not qualified human scientific review.'
