    ### PM-local source records
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-aberg">Aberg et al. (2020). Whole-Grain Processing and Glycemic Control in Type 2 Diabetes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.2337/dc20-0263">10.2337/dc20-0263</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-bruno">Bruno et al. (2024). Weight-neutral early time-restricted eating improves glycemic variation and time in range without changes in inflammatory markers. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2024.111501">10.1016/j.isci.2024.111501</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-jamshed">Jamshed et al. (2019). Early Time-Restricted Feeding Improves 24-Hour Glucose Levels and Affects Markers of the Circadian Clock, Aging, and Autophagy in Humans. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/nu11061234">10.3390/nu11061234</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-karstoft">Karstoft et al. (2017). The effects of 2 weeks of interval vs continuous walking training on glycaemic control and whole-body oxidative stress in individuals with type 2 diabetes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-016-4170-6">10.1007/s00125-016-4170-6</a>. Full publisher PDF Methods, Table 1 and Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-babir">Babir et al. (2026). Exercise snacks performed in real-world settings reduce postprandial hyperglycaemia and glycaemic variability in individuals living with type 2 diabetes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-026-06741-2">10.1007/s00125-026-06741-2</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-trico">Trico et al. (2016). Manipulating the sequence of food ingestion improves glycemic control in type 2 diabetic patients under free-living conditions. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nutd.2016.33">10.1038/nutd.2016.33</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-shukla">Shukla et al. (2017). Carbohydrate-last meal pattern lowers postprandial glucose and insulin excursions in type 2 diabetes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjdrc-2017-000440">10.1136/bmjdrc-2017-000440</a>. Abstract; full methods not recovered in this assessment</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-touhamy">Touhamy et al. (2025). Meal-sequence CGM report (PMID 39688621). <a href="https://doi.org/10.2337/dc24-1956">10.2337/dc24-1956</a>. Publisher PDF denied access; PMC verification barrier; Europe PMC recovery did not yield full methods</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-smith">Smith et al. (2022). Premeal whey protein and free-living glycaemia. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjdrc-2022-002820">10.1136/bmjdrc-2022-002820</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-pabla">Pabla et al. (2025). Medium-chain triglyceride and whey preload crossover trial. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajcnut.2024.12.022">10.1016/j.ajcnut.2024.12.022</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-collins">Collins et al. (2025). Resistant-starch substitution and continuous glucose outcomes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/dme.70079">10.1111/dme.70079</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-sonia">Sonia et al. (2015). Cooling cooked white rice and postprandial glycaemic response. <a href="https://doi.org/10.6133/apjcn.2015.24.4.13">10.6133/apjcn.2015.24.4.13</a>. Primary abstract; full methods not appraised</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-liatis">Liatis et al. (2010). Vinegar reduces postprandial hyperglycaemia in patients with type II diabetes when added to a high, but not to a low, glycaemic index meal. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/ejcn.2010.89">10.1038/ejcn.2010.89</a>. Primary abstract and limited full-text inspection; methods not fully appraised</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-reynolds">Reynolds et al. (2016). Advice to walk after meals is more effective for lowering postprandial glycaemia in type 2 diabetes mellitus than advice that does not specify timing. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-016-4085-2">10.1007/s00125-016-4085-2</a>. Full publisher PDF Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-dempsey">Dempsey et al. (2017). Interrupting prolonged sitting and subsequent glucose regulation in type 2 diabetes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-016-4169-z">10.1007/s00125-016-4169-z</a>. Publisher abstract; full quantitative variability appraisal unavailable</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-bailey">Bailey et al. (2022). Free-living sitting-reduction crossover and glucose outcomes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/nu14030605">10.3390/nu14030605</a>. Full primary Methods/Results</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-resistance">Resistance/endurance comparison (2012). Acute resistance and endurance exercise in insulin-treated type 2 diabetes. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1139/apnm-2012-0494">10.1139/apnm-2012-0494</a>. Primary abstract only</p>
    
    <p id="stage2-ref-wheat">Wheat-porridge comparison (2022). Whole-grain and refined wheat porridge comparison. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/ajcn/nqab434">10.1093/ajcn/nqab434</a>. Primary abstract only</p>
    
