    <Stage2Review>
    <details>
    <summary>Stage 2A/2B evidence and traceability review — 23 October 2026</summary>
    
    **Preserved baseline:** 8 existing Findings, 0 input/participant records, 2 optimisation records and 2 lifestyle records are retained, together with their existing missions, overviews, KCs, citations, Phenome relationships, source assessments and SEC/CC decisions. The manuscript selection does not replace this complete PM.
    
    **Stage 2A — Evidence enrichment:** CGM variability, mean glycaemia, oxidative markers and inferred neuroendocrine consequences remain separate. The longer early-eating/calorie-restriction trial provides contrary context to a different short early/front-loaded protocol, not a reason to delete the existing SOP.
    
    **Stage 2B — Dietary interpretation and traceability:** Both existing SOPs and both lifestyle records are preserved, with their exact statuses. No protocol is promoted to a biochemical input, universal optimal eating window or demonstrated brain intervention.
    
    The standard food dropdown continues to use the existing structured relationships and composition-research ledger. Verified food links, provisional material-specific sources and unresolved pathways remain distinct. No new dietary admission, food edge or score is introduced by this review disclosure.
    
    | Review source | Exact supported proposition | Limits retained | Source |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | wholegrain-processing — primary-human-crossover | Less-processed whole-grain package reduced CGM excursions and variability relative to finely milled nutrient-matched grains. | 31 enrolled, 28 completed; two weeks; type 2 diabetes; combined oats/rice/bread package. Not an isolated fibre effect or brain outcome. | [wholegrain-processing](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7372063/) |
    | early-eating — primary-human-crossover | Six-day early eating with front-loaded calories reduced MAGE and hyperglycaemic exposure in ten adults with prediabetes. | Timing, window and calorie distribution were combined. Inflammatory markers and OGTT did not improve; short trial does not establish universal timing benefits. | [early-eating](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39759025/) |
    | early-eating-null — primary-human-randomised-secondary-analysis | Early time restriction added to calorie restriction did not improve average glucose or insulin-sensitivity measures over calorie restriction alone. | Different 12-week protocol and overweight/obese population; incomplete CGM coverage. Retained as contrary context, not a second independent replication of Bruno. | [early-eating-null](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10804344/) |
    | interval-walking — primary-human-crossover | Two weeks of supervised interval walking reduced CGM MAGE versus the control period in type 2 diabetes. | 14 participants, 13 usable CGM; mean glucose within-period inconclusive; oxidative stress unchanged. Not generic walking-dose or brain-effect evidence. | [interval-walking](https://pure-oai.bham.ac.uk/ws/files/37050575/Karstoft_et_al_The_effects_of_2_weeks_2016.pdf) |
    
    **Outstanding review questions**
    
    No change to a canonical admission is proposed. The exposure, conversion and model limitations above remain part of the bounded evidence account.
    
    </details>
    </Stage2Review>
    
