        decision:
          kc_id: BRS2(KC2)
          ikc_id: BRS2(KC2)
          arm_id: methionine-cysteine-sulfur-amino-acid-pool
          applicability_proposition: Inadequacy of the shared methionine and cysteine pool constrains MAT-dependent SAMe synthesis capacity.
          disposition: unresolved
          established_links:
            - Methionine is the direct amino-acid substrate of MAT (PM3-F1).
          inferred_links:
            - Substrate necessity would have to mean that a lower methionine pool reduces MAT flux in humans.
            - 'Rodent methyl-deficient diets that lower hepatic SAMe also remove choline or replace methionine with homocystine, so they do not isolate this pool.'
          evidence_source:
            finding_ids:
              - PM3-F1
              - PM3-IC1
            citation_keys:
              - bailey_human_mat2a_2021
              - murray_crystallography_2016
              - corrillero_bravo_analysis_2022
          rationale: 'Type D is not met. MAT substrate necessity is established, but pool inadequacy has not been shown to constrain human MAT flux. Hepatic SAMe falls in combined methionine–choline deficiency and in methyl-deficient diets that substitute homocystine for methionine; those designs do not isolate the sulfur amino-acid pool, and SAMe concentration is not MAT flux (PM3-IC1). A four-week vegan versus meat-rich pattern did not separate circulating SAM.'
        rationale: Focused reassessment updates evidence/gap while keeping the stronger constraint unresolved.
        review_record: system/kc-unresolved-decisions-reconciliation.md
