        input: Choline
        input_type: supported upstream supply
        biological_role: >-
          Choline can be oxidised to betaine, supplying methyl groups through [PM2 —
          Betaine/BHMT
          remethylation](/docs/biological-targets/brs2/fm1/brs2-fm1-pm2-betaine-bhmt-remethylation)
          for methionine regeneration upstream of PM3.
        evidence_source:
          finding_ids:
            - PM3-F4
            - PM3-F1
          citation_keys:
            - obeid_metabolic_2013
            - salvi_choline_2013
        evidence_limitation: >-
          Only the choline-to-betaine methyl-supply route is admitted here, not
          choline membrane or neurotransmitter roles. Oxidation, predominantly
          hepatic/renal BHMT activity and alternative supply constrain
          interpretation. Increased MAT flux, increased SAMe availability or benefit
          from additional choline in replete individuals is not established.
        substance_id: choline
        canonical_identity: &ref_3
          status: resolved
          substance_id: choline
          substance_href: /docs/substances/bioactive-compounds/choline-methylation/choline
          verified_against: &ref_7
            - registry/substances.json
            - docs/substances/bioactive-compounds/choline-methylation/choline.md
        kc_id: BRS2(KC1)
        ikc_id: BRS2-KC1-KIT-2
        relationship_type: supported-upstream-supply
        upstream_pm_relationships:
          - relationship_label: Supply
            pm_short_id: PM2
            pm_id: BRS2-FM1-PM2
            href: >-
              /docs/biological-targets/brs2/fm1/brs2-fm1-pm2-betaine-bhmt-remethylation
    dietary_lever_atoms:
      - atom_id: PM3-DIT-1
        requirement_classification: direct
        relationship_layer: dietary-requirement
        dietary_addressability: direct
        claim_ceiling: biological-dependency
        permitted_wording: >-
          Methionine is the Direct amino-acid substrate for MAT-dependent SAMe
          synthesis. This does not establish that increasing methionine intake
          increases MAT flux or SAMe synthesis in adequately nourished humans.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          No ordinary-diet human dose-response demonstrating increased MAT-dependent
          SAMe synthesis was identified.
      - atom_id: PM3-DIT-2
        relationship_layer: biochemical-requirement
        claim_ceiling: biological-dependency
        permitted_wording: >-
          ATP is an intracellular biochemical substrate for MAT-dependent SAMe
          formation. It is not a Dietary Requirement for this PM.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          Diet does not supply ATP to MAT; no dietary ATP lever for SAMe synthesis
          is established.
      - atom_id: PM3-DIT-3
        relationship_layer: biochemical-requirement
        claim_ceiling: biological-dependency
        permitted_wording: >-
          Magnesium ions participate in MAT catalysis under enzyme and structural
          conditions. This does not establish a PM3-specific Dietary Requirement or
          a dietary magnesium effect on human SAMe synthesis.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          No ordinary-diet human evidence demonstrates magnesium-responsive MAT
          flux.
      - atom_id: PM3-DIT-4
        relationship_layer: biochemical-requirement
        claim_ceiling: biological-dependency
        permitted_wording: >-
          Potassium ions participate in MAT catalysis under enzyme and structural
          conditions. This does not establish a PM3-specific Dietary Requirement or
          a dietary potassium effect on human SAMe synthesis.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          No ordinary-diet human evidence demonstrates potassium-responsive MAT
          flux.
      - atom_id: PM3-DIT-5
        requirement_classification: derived
        derived_target: Methionine
        derived_target_atom_id: PM3-DIT-1
        relationship_layer: dietary-requirement
        dietary_addressability: direct
        claim_ceiling: dietary-provision
        permitted_wording: >-
          Dietary protein provides digestible methionine after digestion. This
          provision relationship does not establish that increasing protein intake
          increases MAT-dependent SAMe synthesis.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          Dietary protein provides digestible methionine, but this does not
          establish that increasing protein or methionine intake increases
          MAT-dependent SAMe synthesis in humans. DIAAS evaluates sulfur amino acids
          as methionine + cysteine.
      - atom_id: PM3-KC1-UPSTREAM-1
        relationship_layer: kc-relevance
        claim_ceiling: dietary-provision
        permitted_wording: >-
          Folate supplies 5-methyltetrahydrofolate to [PM1 — Folate/B12-dependent
          remethylation](/docs/biological-targets/brs2/fm1/brs2-fm1-pm1-folate-b12-dependent-homocysteine-remethylation),
          regenerating methionine that PM3 can use to form SAMe.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          This is upstream methionine supply, not a MAT substrate or cofactor. The
          route depends on functional vitamin B12-dependent methionine synthase;
          alternative methionine supply and tissue context affect its contribution.
          Increased MAT flux or benefit from extra folate in replete individuals is
          not established.
      - atom_id: PM3-KC1-UPSTREAM-2
        relationship_layer: kc-relevance
        claim_ceiling: dietary-provision
        permitted_wording: >-
          Betaine donates a methyl group through [PM2 — Betaine/BHMT
          remethylation](/docs/biological-targets/brs2/fm1/brs2-fm1-pm2-betaine-bhmt-remethylation)
          to regenerate methionine used by PM3.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          BHMT activity is concentrated mainly in liver and kidney; this is not
          local BHMT activity in every tissue. Folate-dependent remethylation and
          dietary methionine provide alternative supply. Homocysteine lowering does
          not establish increased MAT flux, increased SAMe availability or benefit
          from extra intake in replete individuals.
      - atom_id: PM3-KC1-UPSTREAM-3
        relationship_layer: kc-relevance
        claim_ceiling: dietary-provision
        permitted_wording: >-
          Choline can be oxidised to betaine, supplying methyl groups through [PM2 —
          Betaine/BHMT
          remethylation](/docs/biological-targets/brs2/fm1/brs2-fm1-pm2-betaine-bhmt-remethylation)
          for methionine regeneration upstream of PM3.
        evidence_limitation: >-
          Only the choline-to-betaine methyl-supply route is admitted here, not
          choline membrane or neurotransmitter roles. Oxidation, predominantly
          hepatic/renal BHMT activity and alternative supply constrain
          interpretation. Increased MAT flux, increased SAMe availability or benefit
          from additional choline in replete individuals is not established.
