    hide_title: true
    scientific_findings_intro: 'Experimental GAD65/GAD67 cofactor-occupancy differences suggest potentially recruitable synthesis capacity. This is not a demonstrated reserve response to ordinary dietary vitamin B6 in the human brain. [[1]](#pm-ref-1) Recombinant human pyridoxal-kinase studies show metal-dependent PLP formation under specified assay conditions, but do not establish physiological zinc limitation. [[2]](#pm-ref-2) [[4]](#pm-ref-4) This evidence supports biochemical dependence, not selective enhancement of brain GABA production from additional vitamin B6 or zinc intake.'
    kc_adjudication_history:
      - date: '2026-10-07'
        decision:
          kc_id: BRS1(KC1)
          ikc_id: BRS1(KC1)
          arm_id: amino-acid-quality
          applicability_proposition: Inadequacy of the shared indispensable-amino-acid quality pool can constrain this mechanism’s governed GABA-synthesis capacity.
          disposition: unresolved
          established_links:
            - Glutamate is the GAD substrate for GABA synthesis (PM9-F1).
            - DIAAS and related scores measure digestible IAA adequacy (FAO 2013; Mariotti 2019).
          inferred_links:
            - Incomplete IAA quality would limit the glutamate pool and thereby constrain GAD capacity.
          evidence_source:
            finding_ids:
              - PM9-F1
            citation_keys:
              - martin_regulation_1993
              - fao_diaas_2013
              - mariotti_dietary_2019
          rationale: Assessment was completed. Quality-scoring evidence does not connect IAA inadequacy to GAD capacity. Glutamate is not an indispensable amino acid. Findings and Dietary Requirements were not reopened.
        rationale: 'Superseded scope: this proposition concerns the former broad indispensable-amino-acid quality arm. Current BRS1 KC1 is the monoamine-precursor LNAA competitive pool. Archiving does not reject general protein-quality biology or alter the separately assessed competitive/supply decisions.'
        review_record: system/kc-unresolved-decisions-reconciliation.md
