    lifestyle_priorities: []
    dietary_confidence_assessments:
      - id: DC-BRS1-FM4-PM9-PM9-DIT-3
        atom_id: PM9-DIT-3
        criteria_version: dietary-confidence-v1
        confidence: High
        type: A
        classification: derived
        proposition: >-
          Dietary vitamin B6 supplies precursors that sustain PLP-dependent glutamate-decarboxylase
          capacity for GABA synthesis.
        scope: >-
          Biochemical capacity under functioning B6 absorption, activation and cofactor handling; not
          quantitative neuronal occupancy, increased GABA from extra intake, or clinical efficacy.
        explanation: >-
          Dietary B6 supplies precursors of the PLP cofactor required by GAD. This is a capacity
          relationship, not evidence that extra B6 increases brain GABA.
        rationale: >-
          High is justified for the defined capacity proposition by convergent GAD enzymology, human B6
          activation enzymology and dietary utilisation evidence. Martin and Rimvall is secondary
          synthesis, not an independent experiment. Human PDXK and PNPO experiments distinguish
          phosphorylation from oxidation; primary nutritional-deficiency evidence supports
          cofactor-dependent brain GAD capacity. Missing trials of extra intake, neuronal PLP occupancy
          or ADHD outcomes do not weaken this narrower established dependency. Plant glucoside
          bioavailability and pathological activation defects limit supply in specified contexts rather
          than negate the relationship.
        essential_links:
          - link: functional_dependency
            status: supported
            statement: >-
              PLP-dependent GAD catalysis is established; dietary deficiency can reduce rat-brain GAD
              activity through cofactor depletion. The proposition is capacity, not increased synthesis
              at sufficient intake.
            sources:
              - https://www.nature.com/articles/nsmb1228
              - https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1471-4159.1972.tb01374.x
          - link: dietary_supply
            status: supported
            statement: >-
              Human tracer studies establish utilisation of oral B6, including incomplete availability
              of PN-glucoside. PDXK and human PNPO experiments support vitamer-specific conversion to
              PLP; this does not measure neuronal cofactor occupancy.
            sources:
              - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1995788/
              - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17766369/
              - https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2323923/
        limitations:
          - >-
            Pyridoxal is phosphorylated to PLP; pyridoxine and pyridoxamine are phosphorylated to
            PNP/PMP and require PNPO-dependent oxidation. This clarification does not edit the preserved
            historical atom.
          - >-
            Food matrices and glycosylated vitamers differ in availability; normal activation cannot be
            assumed in PNPO/PDXK disorders.
          - >-
            No predictable brain GABA response, dose recommendation, benefit above adequacy or ADHD
            efficacy is established; PLP is not GABA-specific.
        finding_links:
          - pm_id: BRS1-FM4-PM9
            finding_id: PM9-F1
            href: /docs/biological-targets/brs1/fm4/brs1-fm4-pm9-gaba-synthesis-capacity#pm9-f1
            explanation: Existing mechanistic Finding; confidence does not inherit its SEC score.
          - pm_id: BRS1-FM4-PM9
            finding_id: PM9-F2
            href: /docs/biological-targets/brs1/fm4/brs1-fm4-pm9-gaba-synthesis-capacity#pm9-f2
            explanation: Existing mechanistic Finding; confidence does not inherit its SEC score.
        review_date: '2026-10-24'
        assessor: Codex — independent bounded scientific review
    
