    <ScientificFinding finding={{"id":"PM9-F2","finding_label":"PDXK metal-nucleotide regulation in vitro","finding_summary":"In recombinant human pyridoxal kinase (PDXK) assays, zinc was the most effective tested divalent cation under the specified in-vitro metal-to-nucleotide conditions. Activity depends on metal–nucleotide speciation and can be inhibited by pyridoxal, ZnATP and excess free zinc. These experiments do not establish physiological zinc limitation, dietary zinc responsiveness or increased human brain GABA synthesis.","finding_interpretation":"This establishes upstream pathway biology only. It does not show that dietary zinc intake, zinc status, or ordinary B6 intake changes human brain GABA synthesis.","finding_statement":"In recombinant human pyridoxal kinase (PDXK) assays, zinc was the most effective tested divalent cation under the specified in-vitro metal-to-nucleotide conditions. Activity depends on metal–nucleotide speciation and can be inhibited by pyridoxal, ZnATP and excess free zinc. These experiments do not establish physiological zinc limitation, dietary zinc responsiveness or increased human brain GABA synthesis.","finding_discriminator":"Concerns formation of the cofactor upstream of the enzyme. PM9-F1 concerns the enzyme's dependence on it.","synthesised_evidence_confidence":"not-yet-scored","synthesis":"Pyridoxal kinase (PDXK) phosphorylates vitamin B6 vitamers. Pyridoxine-5′-phosphate oxidase (PNPO) completes activation of phosphorylated pyridoxine/pyridoxamine to pyridoxal 5′-phosphate (PLP). The experimentally assessed metal/nucleotide effects depend on assay conditions; MgATP evidence prevents a universal zinc-preference claim. PLP is used by many enzyme systems, not selectively by glutamate decarboxylase.","synthesis_limitations":"Pivotal Lee/Navarro full methods and exact uncertainty remain unverified; B/P/R are conditional. No human dietary zinc/B6, physiological PLP-availability or brain GABA effect is established by these assays.","evidence_considered":[{"label":"Lee et al. (2000)","citation_key":"lee_human_2000","href":"/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#lee_human_2000","data_level":"Cellular / Molecular","evidence_source":"bounded-external-search","directional_finding":"In recombinant human PDXK assays, zinc was the most effective tested divalent cation under the specified in-vitro conditions.","assessment":{"study":"Human brain pyridoxal kinase cDNA was isolated and the enzyme overexpressed in E. coli as a maltose-binding-protein fusion, then purified and kinetically characterised.","population":"Recombinant human brain enzyme expressed in bacteria; no tissue or human participants.","result":"Under the specified recombinant-enzyme assay conditions, zinc was the most effective tested divalent cation supporting phosphorylation of pyridoxal. Maximum catalytic activity occurred over a narrow pH range of 5.5 to 6.0. Km values were 97 micromolar for pyridoxal and 12 micromolar for ATP.","effect_magnitude":"Km 97 micromolar (pyridoxal) and 12 micromolar (ATP); relative metal efficacy reported qualitatively rather than as a ratio.","evidence_summary":"Places zinc inside the pathway that forms GAD's cofactor as upstream enzymology.","limitations":"Recombinant enzyme in vitro. The acidic pH optimum and bacterial expression system limit extrapolation to intracellular neuronal conditions, and no zinc-status or intake variable was examined."},"canonical_reference":{"author_year":"Lee et al. (2000)","number":2,"anchor":"#pm-ref-2"}},{"label":"Tang et al. (2005)","citation_key":"tang_crystal_2005","href":"/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#tang_crystal_2005","data_level":"Cellular / Molecular","evidence_source":"bounded-external-search","directional_finding":"Sheep-brain PDXK structural study; contextual binding evidence, not a human zinc-regulation replication.","assessment":{"study":"Crystallographic inhibitor-binding study of sheep-brain pyridoxal kinase.","population":"Purified sheep-brain pyridoxal kinase; no human participants.","result":"Sheep-brain PDXK structural study; contextual binding evidence, not a human zinc-regulation replication.","effect_magnitude":"Structural study; no effect estimate applicable.","evidence_summary":"Sheep-brain PDXK structural study; contextual binding evidence, not a human zinc-regulation replication.","limitations":"Structural and in vitro. The pharmacological focus is the kinase as a drug target; nothing is measured about GABA, brain tissue or physiology."},"canonical_reference":{"author_year":"Tang et al. (2005)","number":3,"anchor":"#pm-ref-3"}},{"label":"Navarro et al. (2013)","citation_key":"navarro_catalytic_2013","href":"/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#navarro_catalytic_2013","data_level":"Cellular / Molecular","evidence_source":"bounded-external-search","directional_finding":"In purified human PDXK assays, several metals supported activity and zinc was the most effective tested cation under the specified in-vitro conditions, but the effect depended on metal-to-nucleotide ratio and the enzyme was inhibited by excess pyridoxal and by zinc-ATP.","assessment":{"study":"Kinetic characterisation of human pyridoxal kinase with explicit determination of the metal-nucleotide species governing activity.","population":"Purified human recombinant enzyme; in vitro kinetics.","result":"Activity was supported by several metals, zinc being the most effective, but the magnitude depended strongly on the relative concentrations of metal and nucleotide. The true substrate is the metal-nucleotide complex; free ATP species did not support activity. The enzyme showed substrate inhibition by pyridoxal and weak inhibition by zinc-ATP.","effect_magnitude":"Reported as kinetic constants across metal-nucleotide species rather than a single comparative effect size.","evidence_summary":"Supplies the concentration and dose constraints on the zinc step, and shows zinc is most effective rather than uniquely required.","limitations":"In vitro kinetics at defined metal and nucleotide concentrations that need not correspond to intracellular conditions. No physiological or dietary variable is tested."},"canonical_reference":{"author_year":"Navarro et al. (2013)","number":4,"anchor":"#pm-ref-4"}}],"evidence_dependency":["All three studies characterise the same enzyme using purified or recombinant protein, so they provide convergent mechanistic evidence rather than independent physiological replication. None supplies physiological or dietary evidence."],"presentation":"mechanistic-basis","sec_assessment_ref":{"id":"gaba-sf2-assessment-v1","version":4,"sha256":"47590e9dd53a15fc7597cd971f66a53ead791beb30143bf51a1b76e4da49f1a7"},"connected_supportive_evidence":[],"informs":[{"phenome":"Emotional Regulation","href":"/docs/phenomes/details/ph003-emotional-regulation"},{"phenome":"Sleep / Calming Tone","href":"/docs/phenomes/details/ph008-sleep-calming-tone"},{"phenome":"Apprehensive Worry / Perseverative Thought","href":"/docs/phenomes/details/ph016-apprehensive-worry-perseverative-thought"}]}} pmId={"BRS1-FM4-PM9"} />
    
